<!-- the Riemann sum calculator used in this Preview is due to Peter Keep -->
<!-- see Peter's original work at https://beta.doenet.org/activityViewer/anvfo1993TKgun9i4sWeLM -->
<!-- a rough draft of this ACS Preview was completed by Jacob Levin after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->

<!-- the Riemann sum calculator used in this Preview is due to Peter Keep -->
<!-- see Peter's original work at https://beta.doenet.org/activityViewer/anvfo1993TKgun9i4sWeLM -->
<!-- a rough draft of this ACS Preview was completed by Jacob Levin after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->

<setup>
  <!-- Calculate the offset: sample point x_i^* = x_i + offset -->
  <!-- Left, Mid and Right Endpoint Rule corresponds to 0, 1, 2 in following computation -->
  <number name="ruleChoiceNumber">
  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Left Riemann Sum">0</case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Midpoint Riemann Sum">1</case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Right Riemann Sum">2</case>
  </conditionalContent>
  </number>
  <number name="offset">$dx*$ruleChoiceNumber / 2</number>
  <!-- numeric version of dx in case symbolic version (displayed to user) slows things down -->
  <math simplify="true" name="dx">($b-$a)/$n</math>
  <number name="dxN">$dx</number>

  <!-- Compute the x_i's here, instead of within the graph -->
  <sequence from="$a" to="$b-$dx" step="$dx" name="xiValues" />
  <!-- For help in zooming into the graph correctly: what are the minimum and maximum rectangle heights? -->
  <!-- make sure to to include 0 in the computations, in case all heights are positive (or negative) -->
  <!-- note: could use $$f($xVal+$offset), but tacking $$f($b) on the computation means less shifting
      of the graph when switching between rules (if rectanges stretch beyond the graph with right 
      endpoints but not left, for example.  Less shifting seems better -->
  <setup><group name="group">$xiValues</group></setup><repeat for="$group" name="fValues" valueName="xVal">$$f($xVal)</repeat>
  <number name="minY"><min>0 $fValues $$f($b)</min></number>
  <number name="maxY"><max>0 $fValues $$f($b)</max></number>
</setup>

<p>
  The graph provided below includes a Riemann sum calculator: in the boxed windows, you can input your own function <m>f(x)</m>, your own interval from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m>, and the number of rectangles, <m>n</m>, you'd like to use.  Then, from the drop-down menu choose your strategy for selecting the <m>x</m>-values from each of the subintervals (a Left, Midpoint, or Right Riemann sum).  The resulting value of the Riemann sum will be computed and displayed below the graph. 
</p>
<p>
  <alert>Note:</alert> you have to hit the <q>enter</q> key after anything you change in one of these boxes in order to update the resulting computations.  Computations involving larger <m>n</m> values can take a few seconds to appear.
</p>

<!-- Start by definining the function, a, b, and n (and hence dx), and rule choice -->
<tabular>
    <row>
      <cell halign="right"><m>f(x) = </m><mathInput prefill="x^2+1" name="fnFormula" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>a =</m><mathInput name="a" prefill="0" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>b =</m><mathInput name="b" prefill="2" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>n =</m><mathInput prefill="6" name="n" /></cell>
      <!--<cell halign="right"><m>\Delta x = <math simplify="true" name="dx">($b-$a)/$n</math></m></cell>-->
    </row>
</tabular>
<tabular>
    <row>
      <cell halign="left">choice for <m>x_k^*</m>:<choiceInput name="ruleChoice" inline="true" preselectChoice="1">
          <choice>Left Riemann Sum</choice>
          <choice>Midpoint Riemann Sum</choice>
          <choice>Right Riemann Sum</choice>
      </choiceInput>
      </cell>
    </row>
</tabular>

<!-- Graph the function, points at (a,0) and (b,0), and then the n rectangles -->
<graph size="medium" xmin="$a-1.5" xmax="$b+1.5" ymin="$minY-1" ymax="$maxY+1" identicalAxisScales="false" showNavigation="false" fixAxes>
  <function name="f" layer="0" symbolic="true">$fnFormula</function>
  <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$f" boundaryValues="$a $b" />

<repeatForSequence from="$a" to="$b-$dxN" step="$dxN" valueName="xVal">
    <polygon stylenumber="6" filled="true" draggable="false" vertices="($xVal,0) 
                ($xVal+$dxN,0) 
                ($xVal+$dxN, $$f($xVal+$offset)) 
                ($xVal,$$f($xVal+$offset))" />
  </repeatForSequence>  
</graph>



<p>
  The value of the Riemann sum is: <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Left Riemann Sum">
      <m>L_{$n}=</m>
    </case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Right Riemann Sum">
      <m>R_{$n}=</m>
    </case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Midpoint Riemann Sum">
      <m>M_{$n}=</m>
    </case>
  </conditionalContent>
  <sum displaydecimals="6">
    <setup><group name="group1">$xiValues</group></setup><repeat for="$group1" valueName="xVal"><number>$$f($xVal+$offset)*$dx</number></repeat>
  </sum>.
</p>

<aside startOpen><title>(a)</title>

<p>
  In the boxes provided above the graph, update the entries to compute a left Riemann sum with <m>n = 5</m> subintervals for the function <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> on the interval <m>[1,4]</m>.  Record the result for <m>L_5</m> in the first row and column of the table below.
</p>

  <p> 
    Then, for this same function and interval, <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> on <m>[1,4]</m>, compute each of <math>L_n, M_n, R_n</math> for <math> n = 5, n = 25</math>, and <math>n = 100</math>, recording your results in the table below. Report the full accuracy provided by the Riemann sum calculator.
  </p>


  <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
    <row bottom="minor">
      <cell right="minor"><m>n</m></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>L_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>M_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>R_n</math></cell>
    </row>
          
    <row>
      
      <cell right="minor">5</cell>
      
      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_5 =</m><answer>16.2</answer>
      </cell>
       
      <cell>
        <m>M_5=</m> <answer>18</answer>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_5=</m> <answer>19.8</answer>
      </cell>
      
    </row>
    
    <row>
    
    <cell right="minor">25</cell>
    
      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{25} = </m><answer> 17.64 </answer>
      </cell>
    
      <cell>
        <m>M_{25} = </m><answer> 18 </answer>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_{25} = </m><answer> 18.36 </answer> 
      </cell>
    
  </row>
    
  <row>
    
      <cell right="minor"><m>100</m></cell>
    
      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{100} = </m><answer> 17.91 </answer>
      </cell>
    
      <cell> 
        <m>M_{100} = </m><answer> 18 </answer>
      </cell>

      <cell> 
        <m>R_{100} = </m><answer> 18.09 </answer>
      </cell>
    
    </row>
  </tabular>
  
</aside>
<aside><title>(b)</title>
<p>
  Observe that the region bounded by <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <m>[1,4]</m> is a trapezoid.  Find the exact area, <m>A</m> of this trapezoid.
</p>

  <p>
    <m>A = </m> <answer>18</answer>
  </p>
</aside>

<aside><title>(c)</title>
  <p>
    Based on your work in (a) and (b), complete the following sentences to summarize what we can observe as we increase the number of subintervals used in the Riemann sum.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> is linear, each midpoint sum <m>M_n</m> results in <choiceInput name="c1" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>an underestimate of</choice>
      <choice>the exact value of</choice>
      <choice>an overestimate of</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>, bounded by <m>f(x)</m> on the interval <m>[1,4]</m>.  In addition, while each left Riemann sum <m>L_n</m> is <choiceInput name="c2" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>an underestimate of</choice>
      <choice>the exact value of</choice>
      <choice>an overestimate of</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>, and each right Riemann sum <m>R_n</m> is <choiceInput name="c3" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>an underestimate of</choice>
      <choice>the exact value of</choice>
      <choice>an overestimate of</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>, as we increase the value of <m>n</m>, we see that the values of both <m>L_n</m> and <m>R_n</m> <choiceInput name="c4" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>stay the same distance away from</choice>
      <choice>get closer and closer to</choice>
      <choice>change without an obvious pattern relative to</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>.
  </p>
<p>
  <answer><award><when>
    $c1.selectedIndices=3 and $c2.selectedIndices=2 and $c3.selectedIndices=4 and $c4.selectedIndices=3
  </when></award></answer>
</p>
    
</aside>
<aside><title>(d)</title>

  <p>
    Above the graph and Riemann sum calculator, update the function to be <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math>, still using the interval <math>[1, 4]</math>. Use the interactive to compute <math>L_n, M_n, R_n</math> for <math> n = 5, n = 25</math>, and <math>n = 100</math> for <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math> on <math>[1, 4]</math>. 
  </p>

  <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
    <row bottom="minor">
      <cell right="minor"><m>n</m></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>L_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>M_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>R_n</math></cell>
    </row>
          
    <row>
      
      <cell right="minor">5</cell>
      
      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_5 =</m><answer>19.68</answer>
      </cell>
       
      <cell>
        <m>M_5=</m> <answer>23.91</answer>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_5=</m> <answer> 28.68 </answer>
      </cell>
      
    </row>
    
    <row>
    
    <cell right="minor">25</cell>
    
      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{25} = </m><answer> 23.1072 </answer>
      </cell>
    
      <cell>
        <m>M_{25} = </m><answer> 23.9964 </answer>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_{25} = </m><answer> 24.9072 </answer> 
      </cell>
    
  </row>
    
  <row>
    
      <cell right="minor"><m>100</m></cell>
    
      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{100} = </m><answer> 23.77545 </answer>
      </cell>
    
      <cell> 
        <m>M_{100} = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.000001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute> 23.999775 </answer>
      </cell>

      <cell> 
        <m>R_{100} = </m><answer> 24.22545 </answer>
      </cell>
    
    </row>
  </tabular>
  
     
  <p>
    What do you conjecture is the exact area, <m>A</m> bounded by <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <math>[1,4]</math>?
  </p>
  <p>
    <m>A = </m><answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>24</answer>
  </p>
  
</aside>

<aside><title>(e)</title>

  <p>
    Why can we not compute the exact value of the area bounded by <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <math>[1,4]</math> using a geometric formula like we did in (b)?  Complete the following sentence to explain 
  </p>
  <p>
    Since <m>f(x) = x^2 + 1</m> is <choiceInput name="e1" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>linear</choice>
      <choice>non-linear</choice>
    </choiceInput> and its curve does not bound a familiar geometric shape (such as a circle), there <choiceInput name="e2" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>is</choice>
      <choice>is not</choice>
    </choiceInput> a known geometric formula to find the area, and thus our best option is to <choiceInput name="e3" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>estimate the area</choice>
      <choice>determine the area exactly</choice>
    </choiceInput>
    by using Riemann sums.
  </p>
  <p>
    <answer><award><when>$e1.selectedIndices=3 and $e2.selectedIndices=3 and $e3.selectedIndices=2</when></award></answer>
  </p>
  
</aside>
